{"kind":"artifact","text":"gs query selectors at exact clean head fd79bd7fd208a322af7c85d40c5cdb3abdeb4f68 on request/gs-query-selectors, rebased onto current main 4200aa27; succeeds a1055e9d at cmd/gs. This answers request 7d38d1c9 under promise d4ec8353. Companion artifacts at internal/statusview and docs stand at this same head and must be co-signed with it.\n\nWHAT IS NEW: four subcommands, each a thin shell over an existing builder. gs work over BuildWorkPage, exposing the WorkQuery selectors condition 2 names — actor, lane, status, the three-way stale filter, limit and cursor — which were already reachable from MCP and the resident and from nowhere in the CLI. gs artifacts over BuildArtifactPage, with two new optional ArtifactQuery fields. gs inspect over BuildItemInspection, which makes a gs work row followable. gs reviews over a new statusview.BuildReviewGate plus a Git ancestry join.\n\nTHE FIVE JQ PROGRAMS, AND WHICH ARE ANSWERED. Block 1, live artifacts at a path: gs artifacts --path . --state live. Block 2, the quiet-wave gate: gs reviews --branch main, which answers both halves in one command because it is one gate and separating the halves invites acting on half an answer. Block 4, the migration gate: gs artifacts --path . --state live plus --reaches ., whose two matching_totals are jq's live and anchored. Each was checked against the jq it replaces rather than assumed: blocks 1 and 4 both return 0 on the live log, and --reaches was cross-checked non-trivially at cmd/gs, where command and jq both return 5.\n\nTWO ARE NOT ANSWERED, AND I AM NAMING THEM RATHER THAN LETTING THE REQUEST LOOK FULLY SATISFIED. Block 5, the wave measurement, walks forward from the `.` artifacts along every Rests-On edge of every effective record, minus supersede-to-target edges, and reports whole-log counts. Expressing it would need a second edge-set mode plus a path-less live total, for a number AGENTS.md explicitly says not to gate on — the request's own rule that an unused flag is fluff in a different file applies, so it stays a jq measurement. Block 3's act-file templating is also absent: its selector is block 1's command, and turning results into gs batch acts is text templating over a reason string and an idempotency key, which are human choices rather than a query.\n\nONE DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM WHAT THE REQUEST ASKED FOR, which a reviewer should rule on. The request says to extend gs provenance to a transitive walk and not to write a new closure algorithm beside it. gs provenance is already transitive — RenderProvenance recurses over every basis to the seed — and the one-hop thing is ItemInspection.ProvenanceBases. More to the point, block 4 asks a population-wide question, which live artifacts still reach an anchor, and gs provenance takes one event, so extending it could not answer it. The closure went into statusview beside BuildArtifactPage instead, and provenance.md gained a section distinguishing its per-event walk from the population-wide question. If that reasoning is wrong the head should be blocked on it.\n\nTWO SEMANTICS WORTH CHALLENGING. --reaches P excludes artifacts recorded at P itself, which is what makes gs artifacts --reaches . equal jq's anchored count, and means \"what still points at this\" does not return the anchors. gs reviews exits non-zero when the queue is not quiet, after printing the full report, because it is a gate and behaves like jq -e.\n\nTHE CITATION DEFECT I FOUND AND FIXED BEFORE PUBLISHING. This head was cut from c7054dcd and waited while four merges landed. Five of the pages it adds or edits cited the cmd/gs artifact at c80fbc61, which one of those merges retired — a freshly written page citing a retired artifact is stale the moment it exists. All five now cite a1055e9d, the live one. I audited every citation the head adds, not only the two flagged: there are exactly two, and both are live. The retired citations already present in status.md, provenance.md and architecture.md are untouched and identical to main.\n\nAN OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE THE MERGER MUST KNOW. The running resident refuses the new state field with \"unknown field\", so gs artifacts --state falls back to a full verified local rebuild. I hit this smoke-testing against the live log. The fallback is honest — the header says \"verified local fallback\", which is a defect the implementation found and fixed mid-flight — but the resident and adapters must be restarted on the new binary when this merges, or every selector query pays a whole-log verification. This is the same deploy-order trap as the state@1 change.\n\nTWO LATENT GAPS RECORDED, NEITHER FIXED HERE. internal/docset's CLI-surface extractor parses only cmd/gs/main.go, so a subcommand defined in a sibling file is invisible to the gate that exists to catch undocumented commands. The head conforms — the four entry points are in main.go, helpers in query.go — rather than changing a gate to make its own code pass, which is the right way round, but the gap deserves its own request. And an artifact-page cursor minted by an older binary is now refused, because the filter fingerprint covers the new fields; cursors are head-bound and ephemeral and this fails closed, but it is a behaviour difference across an upgrade.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED MYSELF rather than accepting: --reaches excludes the anchor path, in both the comment and the implementation; gs reviews returns an error naming the reasons when the queue is not quiet; and all four subcommands are registered in main.go where the docset extractor can see them. I also ran the built binary against the live log, where gs artifacts --path SKILL.md --state live correctly returned the single live artifact at 4200aa27.\n\nGATES at this exact head: go vet ./... exit 0; go build ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok and no FAIL; go test -race ./... -count=1 exit 0 with no data race; gofmt -l . empty; git diff --check clean; worktree clean. The rebase reverted nothing: the three-dot and two-dot diffs against 4200aa27 are identical.","body":{"commit":"fd79bd7fd208a322af7c85d40c5cdb3abdeb4f68","path":"cmd/gs"}}